  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday Night through Friday)\nIssued at 1103 PM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nThe fast moving system will eject out into the Plains Saturday \nnight, resulting in the surface low and associated cold front \nracing eastward as well into Sunday morning. The fast movement of \nthe system will likely limit the moisture return, pushing better \nmoisture further east into the Mississippi Valley. Thus, rain \nchances will be low with this frontal passage. Some low to medium \nshower chances are expected along the front across northwest \nArkansas Saturday night along with some wrap around precip \npossible into the day Sunday mainly north of Highway 412. Thermal \nprofiles support all liquid precip at this point, though can't \nrule out some flurries mixing in as precip wraps up Sunday \nmorning across northwest Arkansas. \n\nThe main impact from this system will be strong wind gusts for a \ntime directly behind the front from early Sunday morning into the\nlate morning hours. Gusts in excess of 40mph appear likely, \nespecially across northeast Oklahoma and thus have issued a Wind \nAdvisory for these locations most likely to see the strongest \nwinds. This advisory could be extended eastward in later updates.\n\nA brief cool down will also follow the frontal passage into early\nnext week, but southerly winds will quickly return, resulting in\nabove normal temps through the middle part of next week. Medium\nrange guidance continues to support a more active pattern\nbeginning to set up across much of the southern CONUS through the\nfirst part of December. The first of this activity will be a\nstronger system slated toward the latter part of next week. Better\nmoisture return with this system could support some severe \nweather potential as well and bears watching in the coming days. \nThe more unsettled pattern looks to last into next weekend as \nwell.\n\nBowlan",
